# Which skills carry a description in the session roster — and are therefore
# available for Claude to auto-invoke without being named by the user.
#
# There is exactly ONE skill tree: .claude/skills/ (reached through the .claude
# symlink into the .claude-setup submodule, which is SHARED ACROSS REPOS — a
# demotion here lands in every repo that uses this setup). Every skill is a real
# directory there. Nothing is symlinked, nothing is copied, and nothing moves
# between directories.
#
# THIS BECAME TRUE ON 2026-10-13. Before that, SEVEN trees existed and drifted:
#   .claude-setup/.claude/skills            (this one, canonical)
#   .claude-setup/.claude-setup/.claude/..  (a submodule nested in the submodule)
#   .cursor/.claude/skills                  (cursor-setup shipped its own copy)
#   .hermes/skills                          (17)
#   features/ai/hermes-agent/skills         (16)
#   features/games/.../idAutomation/.claude-setup/..  (the WoW addon submodule)
#   ~/.claude/skills                        (2, near-empty global tier)
#
# A duplicate tree does not merely waste the roster budget. It RESURRECTS
# DELETED SKILLS: removing the seven hermes-* skills from this tree made Claude
# Code re-register all six name-only ones from .cursor/.claude/skills inside the
# same session, unscoped. It also DOUBLES the roster — discovering
# .cursor/.claude/skills mid-session added ~230 `.cursor:`-prefixed shadows of
# skills already listed here, and the two trees carried contradictory routing
# (that copy of `skill-library` claimed every skill lives in ~/.claude/skills,
# which holds 2). If a second tree ever reappears, the roster is no longer
# governed by this file.
#
# There is no sync script. `sync-skills.sh` was deleted on 2026-10-13: it was
# never wired into hooks.json, and its header documented a tier-2 state of
# "user-invocable-only" (see the warning below) and a `<group>_<subgroup>_<leaf>`
# underscore naming convention. Neither is what this setup uses — 1 of 231
# skills contains an underscore. Running it would have regressed the whole cold
# tier to un-invocable.
#
# This file decides listing cost, not existence. Everything NOT listed here is
# set to "name-only" in .claude/settings.json under `skillOverrides`: the NAME
# still reaches the model, the description does not. Still installed, still
# reachable as /<name>, still readable by path, and — critically — still
# invocable by Claude. It simply cannot be auto-matched, so something has to
# recognise the name. The `skill-library` skill is how.
#
# Do NOT use "user-invocable-only" here. That state hides the skill from the
# model entirely, so Claude cannot invoke it at all — a router can locate a
# skill and still be unable to run it. The whole long tail sat in that state
# until 2026-10-09, which silently broke `skill-library` and `/skills`.
#
# THE BUDGET IS CHARACTERS, NOT ENTRIES.
#
# Claude Code concatenates every rostered skill's name and description into the
# session roster and truncates that string when it overflows — it does not drop
# whole entries, it stops mid-word, and every entry after the cut arrives as a
# bare slug. A skill whose description never reaches the model cannot be
# auto-invoked, so it costs a line and buys nothing.
#
# This was measured, not guessed. With 81 entries totalling 26,629 chars the
# roster truncated around position 73 of 81: `scrutinize` (505 ch) and
# `system-design` (700 ch) arrived bare, while `tdd` at position 78 survived
# purely because its description was 75 chars and still fit. That is a greedy
# byte-fill hitting a ceiling, which sat somewhere near 21,000 chars.
#
# So "90 entries" was the wrong control variable — a roster of 90 short
# descriptions fits comfortably while 81 long ones did not. Budget the sum of
# description lengths, not the entry count.
#
# THE CEILING IS NOW PINNED, NOT INFERRED. As of 2026-10-09 settings.json sets
#
#     "env": { "SLASH_COMMAND_TOOL_CHAR_BUDGET": "30000" }
#
# because the default budget scales with the model's context window — so the
# ~21,000 figure above was never a property of the roster, only of whichever
# model measured it. Pinning it makes the ceiling the same on every model.
#
# Current spend, measured 2026-10-09: ~16,600 chars of rostered descriptions
# (50 entries) plus 3,590 chars of bare names (182 entries) = ~20,200 of 30,000.
# Re-measure with /context (Skills row = post-budget size) and /skill-doctor
# (per-skill cost + invocation counts). Do not trust these numbers after you add
# entries; re-run the audit in `skill-library`.
#
# IDENTITY IS THE DIRECTORY NAME, not the frontmatter `name:`. 28 skills differ
# between the two — `dev-caches-cleanup` declares `linux-dev-caches-cleanup`,
# the `id-effect-*` family declares `id_effect-*`. Key this file and
# `skillOverrides` on the directory name or the override silently fails to match
# and the skill stays on the roster.
#
# The bar for a roster slot:
#
#   1. facts about THIS repo, machine or fleet that cannot be inferred
#   2. version-specific API surface that is easy to misremember
#      (Effect, MikroORM v7, Next 16, Logto)
#   3. the workflow rails — ID, Maestro, tooling contracts
#   4. anything a .claude/commands/*.md invokes by name
#
# What does NOT belong, at any description length: general software craft the
# model performs natively. Commit conventions, rebasing, merge resolution, ADR
# format, clean/hexagonal architecture, DDD theory, generic React guidance,
# generic Playwright guidance, design taste. Those were deleted outright — the
# roster is for things the model cannot derive, not lessons it does not need.
#
# To promote a skill: add its directory name below AND delete its entry from
# `skillOverrides` in settings.json. To demote: the reverse. Nothing moves.

